# Instructions

Classify only the FUNCTION of target source at checks.{{ name }}: is it illustration-associated caption text or a selected excerpt printed with an image, rather than ordinary running narrative?

Use full local_raw_evidence and the image/text positions. A literary excerpt accompanying a photograph can have no Figure label and no quotation marks. A small strip above a nearly full-page image can still be image-associated text. The words may repeat an earlier narrative passage.

Do not decide from image size or text repetition alone. Ordinary body prose can continue across a page with an image. Do not mistake diagram labels, an ordinary chapter heading, a mixed body/caption block or missing text for a whole caption/excerpt. This question asks text role only, not whether a continuation flag is correct.

# True

The whole target text functions as caption/excerpt material associated with the illustration.

# False

That image-associated whole-text function is not established; it may be running prose or another kind of text.
